## Upstream work

This project implements a client for, and a port of parts of,
[Locus](https://github.com/r1n7aro/Locus) — an open-source Unity development
agent by [@r1n7aro](https://github.com/r1n7aro), also released under
GPL-3.0-or-later. It is aligned with Locus commit
[`4ee37ed0`](https://github.com/r1n7aro/Locus/tree/4ee37ed0) (v0.9.3).

Nothing here is an official Locus release, and the Locus authors are not
affiliated with this project.

## Files derived from Locus

### `native/locus_unix_native/` — Linux/macOS broker port

Derived from `crates/locus-native-broker` and `locus_unity_native/` in the
upstream repository. **Modified** for POSIX hosts:

- the Win32 named-pipe transport is replaced by a Unix domain socket server
  (`src/server.rs`, `src/names_unix.rs`);
- the named shared-memory state plane becomes a file-backed `mmap`
  (`src/state_plane.rs`);
- Mono symbol resolution uses `dlopen`/`dlsym` instead of
  `GetModuleHandle`/`GetProcAddress` (`src/mono_names.rs`);
- WebView2, GPU render transport and the in-process background hook have no
  Unix counterpart and return the platform's "unavailable" values (`src/lib.rs`).

The C ABI exported by the library is unchanged, so the unmodified upstream
managed plugin binds it on Linux as it does on Windows.

### `native/locus-native-protocol/` — wire protocol crate

Copied **unmodified** from `crates/locus-native-protocol` upstream. It defines
the newline-delimited JSON envelope, the state-plane byte layout, kernel object
naming, capability identifiers and broker error codes that both sides must agree
on. It is vendored here only so the port builds without an upstream checkout;
the path dependency in `native/locus_unix_native/Cargo.toml` was rewritten to
point at it.

If you intend to modify these files, prefer sending changes upstream so the two
implementations do not drift.

### `src/model-viewer/` — browser three.js model viewer

A JavaScript port of Locus' `src/components/model-preview/modelPreviewEngine.ts`
(GPL-3.0-or-later). It keeps the renderer setup, clip framing, material modes and
statistics, and is adapted to run as a standalone ES-module bundle loaded by the
DSH Sidebar instead of a Vue component. `lib/model-viewer.js` is its generated
bundle; see the bundled third-party notices below for its MIT dependencies.

### `src/skills/` — bundled Unity domain skills

Five Markdown skill documents copied from upstream Locus and **modified only** in
their YAML frontmatter: Locus' `summary`/`tools` fields are replaced by the
`name`/`description` pair this plugin's skill provider serves to DSH, and
`metadata.portedFrom` / `metadata.upstream` record where each body came from. The
bodies are otherwise verbatim:

- `debugger/`, `profiler/` — from upstream `knowledge/skill/`;
- `graphics-debugger/`, `ui-toolkit/`, `view/` — from upstream `skills/`.

Two of them also carry their Unity C# capability sources **verbatim**:

- `graphics-debugger/unity/Editor/` — `FrameDebuggerApi.cs`, `RenderDocCapture.cs`;
- `ui-toolkit/unity/Editor/` — `UIToolkitApi.cs`, `UIToolkitDevTools.cs`.

They are compiled at usage time by the connected editor's own
`compile_skill_package` bridge command (which ships in `payload/locus_unity/`),
so this plugin redistributes source, not binaries. The Python RenderDoc helpers
(`scripts/locus_renderdoc.py`, `scripts/locus_texture_analysis.py`) and the Locus
frontend runtime are **not** redistributed: the first needs RenderDoc and a
Python sidecar this bridge does not provide, the second has no DSH counterpart.

The remaining Locus knowledge documents are not shipped: they only duplicate
DSH's own agent/skill/plugin surface, which DSH already provides.

### `src/sidebar/` and `src/client/` — Sidebar asset panel

**Original work**, not a copy: the right-Sidebar panel and its HTTP routes were
written for this project. They drive the same broker commands the Locus desktop
client drives, and the two behaviours that are Locus' own design decisions are
copied deliberately rather than reinvented:

- a property edit is an in-memory `SerializedProperty` write that dirties the
  object (`LocusBridge.PropertyTree.WritePropertyTree`), not a file rewrite;
- saving follows `LocusBridge.AssetApi.SaveAssetApiScopeAllowed`'s branch order
  (open Prefab Stage → scene → prefab → per-sub-asset flush) in the snippet
  `src/sidebar/unity-asset.js` generates.

No Locus source file is reproduced here, so nothing in this directory carries an
upstream copyright notice. Keeping the *behaviour* aligned is the point: the
panel must not disagree with the editor's own Inspector about what an edit means.

### `payload/locus_unity/` — compiled Unity package

A verbatim, unmodified copy of upstream `locus_unity/` at the commit above,
built so it can be installed into a project. Upstream git-ignores the compiled
assemblies and the native broker, so this tree cannot be reproduced by a UPM
`git+https://…?path=locus_unity` dependency; shipping it is what makes
`unity_status(install:)` install a package that compiles.

It contains, all under GPL-3.0-or-later except where their own notices say
otherwise:

- the Locus editor bridge sources (`Editor/**/*.cs`, `Editor/Locus.Editor.asmdef`);
- merged upstream assemblies `Locus.Roslyn.dll`, `Locus.Json.dll`,
  `Locus.Detour.dll` and `Locus.HotReload.Runtime.dll` (Roslyn, Newtonsoft.Json,
  Mono.Cecil, MonoMod.Detour as recorded in the adjacent `THIRD_PARTY.md` and
  `licenses/` directories inside the package);
- `Editor/Native/x86_64/locus_unity_native.dll`, built **unmodified** from
  upstream's `locus_unity_native` crate at the commit above (release profile
  with MSVC). Its exported symbols equal `locus_unity_native/exports.txt`;
- `Editor/Native/linux-x86_64/liblocus_unity_native.so`, built from this
  repository's `native/locus_unix_native/`.

The tree is regenerated by `scripts/sync-payload.mjs`, never edited by hand.
Every third-party license file inside it travels with the package, so a project
that installs it also receives the notices.

## Bundled third-party code

`lib/model-viewer.js` is generated by `scripts/build-model-viewer.mjs` and bundles
the browser 3D viewer's dependencies into one ES module so the DSH client can
load it without a package manager:

- [three.js](https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js) (`three` 0.185.1) — MIT.
- three.js addons used by the viewer: `OrbitControls`, `RoomEnvironment`,
  `GLTFLoader`, `FBXLoader`, and `MeshoptDecoder`; the decoder embeds
  [meshoptimizer](https://github.com/zeux/meshoptimizer) (MIT, Copyright ©
  2016-2026 Arseny Kapoulkine).
- [fflate](https://github.com/101arrowz/fflate) 0.8.2 (MIT, Copyright © 2023
  Arjun Barrett), bundled inside the three.js FBX loader for decompression.
- [esbuild](https://github.com/evanw/esbuild) is a build-time devDependency
  only; it is not shipped in `lib/`.

